<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.gam.model</string>
  <!-- Hermes chat model (the in-house agents' LLM). ~27GB into RAM, ~1-2 min to load. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <!-- S650: launched through ops/mlx_server.py, which caps the prompt cache
         by BYTES on every path (mlx_lm only honours prompt-cache-bytes when
         batching) and bounds MLX's freed-buffer cache. See that file. -->
    <string>/Users/nicholasrhoades/gam-mlx-env/bin/python3</string>
    <string>/Users/nicholasrhoades/gam/ops/mlx_server.py</string>
    <string>--model</string>
    <string>/Users/nicholasrhoades/models/Hermes-4.3-36B-6bit-mlx</string>
    <string>--host</string>
    <string>127.0.0.1</string>
    <string>--port</string>
    <string>8080</string>
    <!-- S628: CAP THE PROMPT CACHE. Launched without these, mlx_lm.server holds
         every KV cache it has ever built and never evicts one. Measured on
         2026-08-28: 5.5 GB per cached sequence, climbing monotonically —
         0, 5.5, 11.0, 16.5 … 49.9 — and then

           [METAL] Command buffer execution failed: Insufficient Memory
           (kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory)

         The arithmetic is exact on a 96 GB machine: the model is 27.7 GB
         resident, the default GPU wired limit is about 72 GB, and 27.7 + 51
         does not fit. It died twice in fifteen minutes, roughly every ninth
         conversation, and restarted clean at 0 GB each time.

         THIS IS WHY PACING NEVER HELPED. S626 added idle gaps between eval
         cases after the kernel panics, reasoning about cadence. The variable is
         not time, it is the NUMBER of distinct sequences cached — a job spread
         over three hours OOMs at exactly the same request count as one run
         flat out. That is also why the panics landed mid-run rather than
         between jobs.

         16 GiB is about three sequences: enough that a follow-up turn still
         hits a warm cache, with ~28 GB of headroom for the OS, WindowServer and
         anything else on the GPU. Eviction costs a prompt re-computation, which
         is slower and never wrong. -->
    <!-- S628: --prompt-cache-SIZE is the one that works. It is applied where the
         LRU is constructed (server.py:1743), so it binds on EVERY code path.
         --prompt-cache-bytes is applied at server.py:798, inside the BATCHED
         path only — these requests are single and sequential, take
         _serve_single, and never reach that trim. Set to 16 GiB and the cache
         still climbed to 44.7 GB.

         The default size is 10, and 10 is exactly where it died every time:

             10 sequences, 39.79 GB
             10 sequences, 45.57 GB
             10 sequences, 51.05 GB
              0 sequences,  0.00 GB   <- Metal OOM, launchd restart

         Capped by COUNT and not by bytes, so ten conversations at ~5 GB each
         overruns a 96 GB machine holding a 27.7 GB model under a ~72 GB wired
         limit.

         SIX, to match AGENT_MAX_CONCURRENT in concurrencyGate.ts — one warm
         cache per conversation the gate will admit, so nobody loses their cache
         mid-conversation and has their prompt reprocessed. 6 x 4.51 GB worst
         case is 27 GB, plus the model is 55 GB, comfortably inside the limit.

         This is also what makes Nic's "when the conversation is done it
         disappears" true: the seventh conversation evicts the first instead of
         the server dying. -->
    <string>--prompt-cache-size</string>
    <string>6</string>
  </array>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>GAM_MLX_PROMPT_CACHE_GB</key>
    <string>20</string>
    <key>GAM_MLX_BUFFER_CACHE_GB</key>
    <string>4</string>
  </dict>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/gam-mlx.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/gam-mlx.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
